export function once(fn) {
  // TODO: remember whether fn already ran, and its result
}

fund-js/02-scope-hoisting-and-closures/0.solution:
export function once(fn) {
  let called = false;
  let result;
  return function (...args) {
    if (!called) {
      called = true;
      result = fn.apply(this, args);
    }
    return result;
  };
}

fund-js/02-scope-hoisting-and-closures/0.explanation:
A flag and the stored result live in the closure; a regular `function` wrapper keeps the caller's `this`:

```js
export function once(fn) {
  let called = false;
  let result;
  return function (...args) {
    if (!called) {
      called = true;
      result = fn.apply(this, args);
    }
    return result;
  };
}
```

Testing `result === undefined` instead of a separate flag fails the test "a first result of undefined still counts as the call (boundary)": a function that returns undefined would run again on every call. An arrow wrapper fails "arguments and this reach the wrapped function", because an arrow takes `this` from the scope it was written in rather than from the call `obj.go('!')`, so `this.name` is not 'pay'. `fn.apply(this, args)` forwards both the receiver and the arguments in one call.
